+Building an `RDBStorage` initialises the file it opens, and not atomically: `metadata.create_all` looks
+for each table and then creates it, and `_VersionManager` adds the version row and stamps the alembic
+revision when it finds none (`storages/_rdb/storage.py` at v5.0.0, installed). Two processes doing so at
+once on a new file collide ("table studies already exists"). So each storage is built while holding an
+exclusive `flock` on the folder that holds the file (Python 3.14's `fcntl.flock`, flock(2) on macOS and
+Linux): opening a folder creates nothing, the kernel drops the lock if its process dies, and the lock is
+released as soon as the storage is built. No fd of the SQLite file itself is opened for it, since
+closing one would drop SQLite's own POSIX locks (https://www.sqlite.org/howtocorrupt.html, section
+2.2; not re-read 2026-11-11, UNVERIFIED).
